    "title": "Trusted binary spawned a shell (GTFOBin)",
    "desc": "A trusted, non-shell utility spawned an interactive system shell - the signature \"living off the land\" move catalogued by GTFOBins (the Linux analog of LOLBAS). find's -exec, tar's --checkpoint-action, awk's BEGIN{system()}, busybox's shell sub-command, or a scanner/debugger forking /bin/sh all run a shell through a binary that is on the box for another reason, so the shell blends into ordinary tool use (MITRE T1202 Indirect Command Execution / T1059.004 Unix Shell; T1218 is the Windows-canonical \"system binary proxy execution\" this mirrors). Two signals: the recipe in the utility's own argv (an -exec/checkpoint/BEGIN{system escape naming a shell), which is what the raw-auditd floor sees since it carries no ParentImage; and the parent/child shape (a curated set of utilities that never legitimately fork a shell, seen as ParentImage of a shell) where Laurel/mimi/Sysmon populate the parent. reverse_shell owns the socket-back-out form; this owns the local shell break-out.",
